  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-16T07:52:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "f9ae71d5-60ba-478f-bb62-35388d591710",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 160752\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1252 AM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north to northeast winds this morning will diminish through \n  the afternoon.\n\n* Precipitation chances return to the region early to middle of\n  next week, with significant uncertainty in forecast and impact\n  details.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the Middle of Next Week.\n\nThrough the overnight, gusty northeast winds were reports across \nportions of the area at times. The strongest winds were in the \nterrain with gusts over 40 MPH reported as higher elevations \nintercepted a 50KT+ 850-700mb jet. This lower level jet associated \nwith a low pressure system sitting over southern California will \nremain draped over southern portions of the region through rest of \ntonight before slowly shifting south and weakening this morning. \nWhile gusts are fairly isolated at the moment, expecting a quick \nuptick in the valleys early this morning as better mixing occurs \nwith sunrise as well as an area of better vorticity advection on the \nbackside of the low. A Wind Advisory remains in effect through 10AM \nthis morning which looks good as additional minor wind impacts are \npossible with the uptick in winds after sunrise. Winds will diminish \nthis afternoon as the low shifts south and weakens. Widespread gusts \n20-30 MPH are expected this afternoon, then gusts under 20 MPH are \nexpected through at least Monday. After a brief warm up to near \nnormal today, temperatures drop a few degrees back to slightly below \nnormal levels for the weekend. \n\nContinuing to watch the potential for weather impacts around midweek \nas a deep West Coast trough impacts the region. Strong forcing will \nbe associated with this deepening low, which some models have \nclosing off as it moves into central or southern California. This \nforcing will interact with an uptick in moisture that advects into \nthe region from distant Tropical Storm Simon ahead of the trough. \nCluster analysis shows significant variation in how quickly the \ntrough moves into the region as well as how deep it will be once it \ndoes so. In addition, models continue to advertise differing \nsolutions on how an IVT plume sets up somewhere over southern Nevada \nor western Arizona. All that to say- there is significant \nuncertainty in the forecast details and potential impacts the middle \nof next week. It's fair to say precipitation is expected at some \npoint after Monday, with snow possible above 8000-9000ft, but rain \nimpacts will not be as significant as what just occurred with the \nremnants of Hurricane Rachel. There also should be an increase in \nwinds ahead of the trough, likely Tuesday, which could result in \nwind impacts. Low confidence though in exact timing of rain, where \nthe highest impacts will occur, and how much snow to expect in the \nhigher elevations.With the incoming system and the potential for \nrain, temperatures will cool and remain below normal early next week \nwhen the system moves inland.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Clear \nskies with breezy to strong northerly winds last through the TAF \nperiod. There may be few hour period later tonight and into the \nearly morning hours where gusts diminish, but expect sustained winds \nto remain elevated 6-12 kt.  Strong northeast winds (020 to 040 deg) \nreturn around or after 16z with gusts approaching 30 kt during the \nafternoon and evening. Gusts and winds begin a diminishing trend \nduring the evening, with gusts likely dropping off after sunset.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Clear skies and breezy \nconditions continue into Friday for most terminals. Strong North to \nNortheast winds with gusts over 30 kt at times persist into tonight \nfor the Colorado River Valley and LV Valley sites. KIFP and KEED \nkeep strong gusts well into tomorrow while LV Valley sites may see a \nbrief period of lighter winds late tonight into early tomorrow \nbefore increasing after 16z. Light and variable winds tonight for \nKBIH become southerly tomorrow afternoon gusting over 20 kt at times \nbefore sunset. KDAG will favor more of a NE to ENE direction with \ngusts also to 30 kt Friday afternoon.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
